{
  // pdn-soundmodem example configuration (JSON with comments allowed).
  // Start with: pdn-soundmodem --config soundmodem.json
  "device": "default",
  // A FlexRadio over the LAN is a device too: "flex:<radio>[:slice][@station]" - <radio> is
  // `discover`, an IP (host[:port]), a discovery spec (serial=…/name=…) or `mock`. With no
  // @station the daemon OWNS the radio and brings it up headless (creates its own slice - the
  // "flex" section below sets its frequency/antenna/mode). A trailing @station attaches to a
  // running SmartSDR's slice instead. The Flex keys itself, so omit "ptt". e.g.:
  //   "device": "flex:10.45.0.76",            // headless, slice A, 14.100000 MHz / ANT1 / DIGU
  //   "device": "flex:10.45.0.76:A@Flex",     // attach to SmartSDR station "Flex", slice A
  // The headless path disables band persistence and explicitly tunes the created slice, so it
  // lands on "frequency" regardless of the radio's last-used band. "daxChannel" (default "1")
  // applies to both paths; sharing a box with a running SmartSDR (which grabs DAX 1) needs a
  // different channel here (or use attach mode). See docs/flex-integration.md §8.
  // "flex": { "antenna": "ANT1" },   // mode defaults to DIGU; daxChannel defaults to 2 headless,
  //                                  which keeps out of a running SmartSDR's way (it takes 1)
  // The radio's transmit filter is global and persistent, so a headless station sets its high
  // cut from what the modems occupy rather than inheriting whatever the rig was left on (a 3000
  // Hz default clips ms110d-*, which reaches ~3.2 kHz). "transmitFilterHighHz" pins it; 0 leaves
  // the radio's own filter alone.
  // A public UberSDR web receiver is a device too: "ubersdr:<instance>" - a host, a host:port,
  // or the https:// URL you would open in a browser. The daemon takes the receiver's IQ stream
  // and demodulates SSB from it in-process, so every mode, the waterfall and the frame log work
  // as they do on a sound card. It is RECEIVE ONLY: there is no transmitter, so omit "ptt", and
  // transmissions are refused rather than queued. Give the modems "rfFrequency" (or pin
  // "dialFrequency") - that is what tunes the receiver. e.g.:
  //   "device": "ubersdr:m9psy-1.instance.ubersdr.org",
  //   "ubersdr": { "mode": "iq48" },   // also: password, ssbLowHz/ssbHighHz, gain
  // For a public monitor page, "onDemand": true holds a session on the receiver only while
  // somebody has the waterfall open ("lingerSeconds", default 60, keeps it across a page
  // refresh); pair it with "public": true in "waterfall" below.
  "captureRate": 48000,
  // Modems can be placed by absolute RF frequency instead of audio centre - state the band
  // plan the way you think of it and the daemon works out the dial, tells you what to set,
  // and refuses to start if no single dial can fit them all. See CONFIG.md.
  //   "sideband": "usb",            // or "lsb"; RF = dial + audio on USB
  //   "dialFrequency": 7049450,     // omit and the daemon chooses one that fits
  //   "modems": [ { "subChannel": 0, "mode": "bpsk300", "rfFrequency": 7051600 } ]
  // The shared KISS port: every modem, selected by the KISS port nibble. A modem can also be
  // given a port to itself with "port" in its entry below - that port carries only that
  // modem, presented as nibble 0, which is what host software that hardcodes KISS channel 0
  // needs in order to reach a modem that is not sub-channel 0.
  "kissPort": 8105,
  // "frequency" (optional) sets a modem's audio centre in Hz, QtSoundModem-style, on both
  // TX and RX - e.g. move 300 BPSK to 1459 to meet a peer sitting off the usual centre.
  // It applies to the AFSK tone-pair modes (afsk*, default 1700) and the BPSK/QPSK carrier
  // modes (bpsk*/qpsk*, default 1500; 1650 for qpsk3600). The baseband FSK families
  // (fsk*/c4fsk*) and the spec-fixed waveforms (freedv-*/ms110d-*) have no settable centre -
  // a "frequency" on those is rejected at start-up. Omit the field to use the mode default.
  //
  // bpsk300/bpsk1200 default to a differential frequency-diversity bank - parallel decoder
  // branches at stepped centres (differential detection matches/beats coherent on real off-air
  // HF, benchmarked against a NinoTNC). "offsetPairs" (branches either side of centre) and
  // "offsetStepHz" (Hz between branches) tune coverage - ±offsetPairs·offsetStepHz; omit for
  // the defaults (4 pairs, baud/40 step). offsetPairs:0 gives a plain single modem.
  // --psk-detector coherent forces coherent (QPSK stays coherent regardless). bpsk300-multi /
  // bpsk1200-multi are aliases of bpsk300 / bpsk1200.
  //
  // The afsk300 family (afsk300/afsk300-il2p/afsk300-il2pc) also defaults to a diversity
  // bank - tight ±250 Hz branches stepped across the offset range, because on a crowded HF
  // band the receive filter has neighbours to reject, and narrow branches plus diversity
  // beat one wide filter (measured off-air 2026-08-02: 3/13 frames → 13/13 on the live 40 m
  // slot). Same knobs: defaults 5 pairs at 35 Hz (±175 Hz); offsetPairs:0 gives a single
  // tight-filtered modem.
  //
  // Every IL2P+CRC modem also reads plain IL2P (no trailing CRC), for a neighbour that sends
  // the CRC-less variant - a BPQ32 node, typically, which was otherwise a perfectly
  // demodulated burst that produced nothing at all. Those frames are always shown: badged
  // "RS ONLY" on the waterfall panel, in the frame log with crc_valid null, and on the journal
  // line. They are NOT passed to the KISS host unless you ask, because Reed-Solomon alone is
  // what IL2P+CRC exists to improve on - expect the occasional frame of rubbish on a noisy
  // channel. Receive only either way; it does not change what you transmit. Per modem:
  //   { "subChannel": 1, "mode": "bpsk300", "frequency": 2150, "acceptPlainIl2p": true }
  "modems": [
    { "subChannel": 0, "mode": "afsk1200-multi", "frequency": 1700 },
    { "subChannel": 1, "mode": "bpsk300", "frequency": 1500, "offsetPairs": 4, "offsetStepHz": 7.5 }
    // Add "port": 8110 to an entry to give that modem a TCP port of its own.
  ],
  "ptt": { "type": "cm108", "device": "/dev/hidraw0", "gpio": 3 },
  // The sound card's own mixer (optional). Every key is optional and LEAVING ONE OUT LEAVES
  // THAT CONTROL EXACTLY AS THE CARD HAS IT - which is why nothing here is set by default: the
  // card is whatever alsamixer, a reboot or a re-plug last left it, and this is for pinning the
  // ones that matter. THE LEVELS ARE IN dB, inside the card's own range, which differs by card:
  // `pdn-soundmodem --mixer-show hw:1` prints the range and the control names (it only reads,
  // and works while the station is running). A CM108 is typically -12 to +23 dB on capture and
  // -37 to 0 dB on playback. A level outside the range is journalled with the range and that
  // one control is left alone; a card that publishes no dB scale at all says so and is left
  // alone too. Recommended for a data modem: "agc": false (automatic gain fights the modem's own
  // level tracking and turns the noise floor into a moving target) and "micBoost": false unless
  // the radio's output is genuinely low. Many cards have no mic boost control at all - a CM108
  // folds it into the top of the capture range - and the journal says so and carries on.
  //
  // Anything set here is applied at every start-up and WINS over a change made on the operator
  // page. A page change to a control this block does not mention is remembered in
  // mixer-state.json (in the systemd state directory, /var/lib/pdn-soundmodem for the package)
  // and applied at the next start-up; "stateFile" moves that file somewhere else.
  // "alsa": { "mixer": { "captureGainDb": 6.0, "agc": false, "micBoost": false, "playbackDb": -8.0 } },
  // Browser waterfall (optional): 30 fps spectrum + waterfall at http://<bind>:<port>/,
  // every modem's measured band overlaid (audio + RF centres), each decoded frame tagged
  // on its burst with callsign, SNR and frequency offset. "dialFrequencyHz" presets the
  // rig dial the RF scale derives from (0 = unset; every browser can retune its own copy);
  // "sideband" is "usb" or "lsb"; "bind" "*" opens it beyond loopback.
  // "waterfall": { "port": 8107, "dialFrequencyHz": 14105000, "sideband": "usb" },
  // "public": true dresses the page for strangers rather than the operator: a "title", an
  // "about" paragraph, a credit and link for the web receiver it listens through, and no KISS
  // host badges. Nothing else changes.
  // "waterfall": { "port": 8099, "public": true, "title": "40 m packet monitor", "about": "..." },
  // POCSAG paging endpoint (optional; DAPNET/POCSAG-compatible waveform, local paging
  // API - line-based TCP: "PAGE <ric> <function> ALPHA|NUMERIC|TONE [text]").
  // "paging": { "port": 8106, "baud": 1200, "invertPolarity": false },
  // ARDOP virtual TNC (optional; ardopcf-compatible TCP host interface - Pat and
  // other Winlink hosts connect unmodified; data port is always port+1). The ARDOP
  // channel is dedicated: "ardop" is exclusive with "modems"/"paging", and the
  // channel must run at 12 kHz). Preferred form is a modem entry, which also takes a
  // "frequency" (ARDOP is pinned to 1500 Hz; the daemon shifts it) and shares the passband
  // with the packet modes - while an ARQ session is up their transmissions are held:
  //   { "subChannel": 1, "mode": "ardop", "frequency": 950, "port": 8101 }
  // "port" there speaks the ardopcf host interface, not KISS, and reserves port+1 for data.
  // "ardop": { "port": 8515 },
  // Channel access (TXDELAY, P, SLOTTIME, TXTAIL) is NOT configured here - it belongs to the
  // host, which sets it at runtime with the KISS parameter commands 0x01-0x04. Until a host
  // does, the defaults are 300 ms / 63 / 100 ms / 20 ms. `--txdelay MS` overrides the TXDELAY
  // default for bench runs with no host attached.
  // One bind for every listener: KISS, the per-modem ports, the waterfall, paging and ARDOP.
  // Loopback unless you say otherwise - KISS has no authentication.
  // Every frame heard, written to SQLite (WAL, so you can read it while the modem runs).
  // Omit and nothing is written down.
  // "frameLog": { "path": "/var/lib/pdn-soundmodem/frames.db" },
  // Dead-feed protection: the daemon restarts itself when the input stops delivering samples
  // at all (every device) or delivers unbroken digital silence (flex/ubersdr by default; off
  // for ALSA, where genuinely-silent wired inputs exist). Thresholds in seconds, 0 = off;
  // omit for per-device defaults. See CONFIG.md § deadFeed.
  // "deadFeed": { "silenceSeconds": 30, "starvationSeconds": 30 },
  //
  // Offer this station to a public monitor site. One block, plus a token the site owner issues,
  // and this station appears alongside the web receivers at monitor.ukpacketradio.network with
  // its own page: the waterfall, the AX.25 links panel, the frames it decoded and a Listen
  // button. The station DIALS OUT - no port to open, no certificate, no DNS - and nothing flows
  // until somebody is watching. STRICTLY ONE WAY: the only message the site sends back is how
  // many people are watching, so nothing there can transmit, retune or reconfigure anything
  // here, and a site that is down never faults this station. 16-bit PCM, no codec, so what a
  // visitor hears is modem audio sample for sample: 194 kbit/s upstream while somebody is
  // watching at the default 12000, under 1 kbit/s while nobody is, and one stream however many
  // are watching. ADSL uploads will feel that; "audioRate": 6000 halves it at the price of a
  // 0-3 kHz picture. Leaving is deleting this block and restarting. Full reference: CONFIG.md
  // § publish. Mutually exclusive with "monitor" below.
  //
  // "publish": {
  //   // The site's uplink endpoint and the token it issued you. Both are given to you
  //   // together; paste the token in as it was given and do not edit it by hand.
  //   "url": "wss://monitor.ukpacketradio.network/uplink",
  //   "token": "pdnsm_...",
  //   // Required: a station on a public page has to say whose it is, and the site checks this
  //   // against the token. The rest is what the credit line and the picker row are made of.
  //   "callsign": "GB7RDG-2",
  //   "operator": "Tom M0LTE",
  //   "location": "Reading, England",
  //   "radio": "IC-7300 into a doublet at 10 m",
  //   "site": "https://gb7rdg.example/",
  //   // The rate the audio is relayed at: an integer divisor of the channel's DSP rate, 6000
  //   // to 48000. The relayed waterfall spans 0 to half of it, so a 48 kHz station leaving the
  //   // default gets a 0-6 kHz picture and start-up says which modems fall outside it.
  //   "audioRate": 12000,
  //   // "always" (the default) sends decoded frames whether or not anybody is watching, which
  //   // is what makes a quiet band look alive to somebody arriving an hour later; "watched"
  //   // holds them back.
  //   "frames": "always"
  // },
  //
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The OTHER thing this daemon can be. Everything above describes one station: one radio or
  // one web receiver, one KISS port, one page. A "monitor" section instead makes it one site
  // fronting MANY UberSDR web receivers, with a front page listing them and a visitor picking
  // one. Same binary, same package; the configuration is the switch, and "monitor" is
  // mutually exclusive with "device". Receive only, and there is no KISS, no PTT, no config
  // API and no survey in this flavour - a monitor is a display. Full reference: CONFIG.md
  // § monitor. Delete "device", "kissPort", "modems" and "ptt" above and uncomment this:
  //
  // "monitor": {
  //   // This site's own address as the world reaches it, scheme and host (and port if it is
  //   // not the scheme's), nothing after them. Leave it out and it is worked out from each
  //   // request, which is right until something in front rewrites the Host header - a tunnel
  //   // leaves "127.0.0.1:8099" here, and then the only thing that knows the public name is
  //   // this line. It is what a relayed station is told its own page is at, so its journal
  //   // reads "publish: live at https://monitor.ukpacketradio.network/r/gb7rdg-2/".
  //   "publicUrl": "https://monitor.ukpacketradio.network",
  //   // Where the list of receivers comes from. Fetched again every "refreshMinutes".
  //   "directory": "https://instances.ubersdr.org/api/instances",
  //   "refreshMinutes": 5,
  //   // How long each receiver's session is held after the last viewer of THAT receiver
  //   // leaves. One session per receiver, however many people are watching it.
  //   "lingerSeconds": 60,
  //   // "allow" non-empty lists only those hosts - which is how you run this against two
  //   // receivers for a smoke test. "deny" is how an operator who asks not to be listed is
  //   // not listed, and it beats "allow". Both match the directory's "host", case-insensitively.
  //   "allow": [],
  //   "deny": [],
  //   // The modems every receiver is given, same schema as "modems" above. Give them all an
  //   // "rfFrequency": a web receiver has no dial already set to read off, so the band plan
  //   // is the only thing that can tune it. An "ardop" entry is accepted and would draw its
  //   // band on every receiver's waterfall, but this flavour runs no virtual TNC, so nothing
  //   // would ever decode there; slot 1 is GB7RDG-2's ARDOP one and is left out for that
  //   // reason, the numbering kept so that 0 and 2 here are 0 and 2 on the node.
  //   "modems": [
  //     { "subChannel": 0, "mode": "afsk300-il2pc", "rfFrequency": 7050300 },
  //     { "subChannel": 2, "mode": "bpsk300",       "rfFrequency": 7051600 }
  //   ],
  //   // The other end of "publish": private stations this site accepts an uplink from, listed
  //   // in the same picker as the receivers and tagged "station". Empty (the default) accepts
  //   // none and /uplink is a 404. Run "pdn-soundmodem --uplink-token GB7RDG-2" to mint one
  //   // station's token: it prints the token and the whole entry to paste in here, writes
  //   // neither anywhere, and you keep the hash and give the token to that station's operator
  //   // once. The callsign is bound to the token, the slug never comes off the wire, and
  //   // removing an entry needs a restart.
  //   "uplinks": [
  //     {
  //       "callsign": "GB7RDG-2",
  //       "slug": "gb7rdg-2",
  //       "tokenSha256": "9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015a3bf4f1b2b0b822cd15d6c15b0f00a08"
  //     }
  //   ]
  // },
  // // The picker is served at "/" on this port and each receiver's page under "/r/<slug>/".
  // // "public" is forced true here: a picker is a page for strangers by definition.
  // "waterfall": {
  //   "port": 8099,
  //   "title": "UK packet monitor",
  //   "about": "The 7050-7052 kHz packet window on 40 m, as heard by public web receivers. Pick a receiver to watch. Receive only: this site decodes what it hears and shows the AX.25 links and frames; nothing is transmitted."
  // },
  // // A DIRECTORY under "monitor", not a file: one frames-<slug>.db per receiver inside it.
  // "frameLog": { "path": "/var/lib/pdn-soundmodem" },
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "bind": "127.0.0.1"
}
